A row of a reactive loop holds its own schedule.

Per-iteration state has one home in this language: the record a live instance
gets. A row of a reactive `for` is such an instance, so the handle html's
`timer` override keeps is a local of that row's factory call and no two rows
share one. Nothing about `effect` knows this, which is the point -- the same
answer serves any construct whose state has to be per iteration.

Three things had to be true for the factory to exist at all, and none of them
was:

  - The widening that puts imported components on pkg.Components judged an
    overridden stdlib component bodiless, because it runs before
    passPlatformExtensionBody swaps the override in. Never on the list, never
    lowered, no declaration for the factory emitter to read.
  - The pass that takes those components back off again deleted this one, since
    by then the node that elected it is a CreateComponent call rather than an
    ir.NodeInst and the second reachability walk finds nothing.
  - Specializing an override is a *reset* of Body and Vars, and a build
    optimizes again after lowering. The second swap discarded the promoted
    event cell and the whole settle chain, so the factory came out reading
    `__prop_tick`, a name nothing declared.

And `#[construct]` on `interval` is what makes a row rebuildable rather than
patchable: a running schedule cannot have its period written into it, so a row
whose period changed is destroyed and built again. It is the missing *setter*
that says so. The `__prop_interval` cell beside it is not a way to write one in
-- it is how `__effect0_down` reads the prop at all, the parameter being out of
scope in a function of the row rather than of its factory call. On the four platforms whose
timer is still a primitive the mark is never consulted: the node is a schedule
codegen reads, and nothing about it is a prop reactivity patches.

deny out/none/html/index.html `state.handle` -- the handle is a row's own local, never page state
deny out/none/html/index.html `__set_interval` -- a period is not written into a live schedule; the row is rebuilt

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import . "sngl:time"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
}

component main node {
    var ticks = 0
    var periods list<duration> = [20ms, 500ms]

    button(text="add", @click { periods.push(1000ms) })
    for var p = periods {
        timer(interval=p, enabled=true, @tick { ticks += 1 })
    }
    text(value="ticks=" + string(ticks))
}

window {
    main
}
